{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully. I need to implement a `solve(case)` function that performs grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree.\n\nKey rules from the problem:\n1. Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes (process deeper nodes first)\n2. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\n3. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n4. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\n5. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nLet me think about the algorithm:\n\n1. Parse the input nodes\n2. Build a tree structure (parent-child relationships)\n3. Process nodes in order: deeper first, ties broken by node ID (lexicographic?)\n4. For each node (in that order):\n   - Skip if not removable\n   - Skip if it's root\n   - Skip if its symbol is required (required_symbols)\n   - Skip if it has retained children\n   - Tentatively remove it; check if all required symbols are still represented in retained nodes\n   - If yes, mark as removed\n\nWait, re-reading: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\"\n\nSo the conditions to skip removal:\n- Node is root (parent is null) OR\n- Node's symbol is in required_symbols OR\n- Node has retained children\n\nAnd additionally: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\"\n\nLet me look at the fixture data more carefully:\n\nFor case fg1-se-pe-01-b-000:\n- required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- Nodes:\n  - root (depth 0, removable: false, symbol: root)\n  - n1 (depth 1, removable: false, symbol: s1)\n  - n2 (depth 1, removable: true, symbol: s2)\n  - n3 (depth 1, removable: false, symbol: s0)\n  - n4 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s1)\n  - n5 (depth 2, removable: false, symbol: s2)\n  - n6 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s0)\n\nProcessing deeper nodes first (depth 2 before depth 1):\nAt depth 2: n4, n5, n6\n\nTie-breaking by node ID. Node IDs are \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\" - lexicographic ordering.\n\nFor n4 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s1):\n- Symbol s1 is in required_symbols → skip (cannot remove)\n\nFor n5 (depth 2, removable: false, symbol: s2):\n- Not removable → skip\n\nFor n6 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s0):\n- Symbol s0 not in required_symbols\n- No children (leaf node)\n- If removed, check required symbols: root is represented by \"root\" node, s1 is represented by n1 (symbol s1) and n4 (symbol s1)\n- All required symbols still represented → remove n6\n\nAt depth 1: n1, n2, n3\n\nFor n1 (depth 1, removable: false, symbol: s1):\n- Not removable → skip\n\nWait, but the check is: never remove if removable=false? Or is removable a property that affects whether we try?\n\nLooking at the problem again: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\"\n\nThe `removable` field seems to be part of the grammar-validity check. So a node with `removable: false` cannot be removed.\n\nSo the conditions for attempting removal:\n1. removable must be true\n2. Symbol must NOT be in required_symbols (the node's symbol is required)\n3. Node must not have retained children\n4. After tentative removal, all required_symbols must still be covered\n\nWait, but n1 has symbol s1 which IS in required_symbols. And n4 also has symbol s1. So even if n1 is not removable, n4 has symbol s1 too.\n\nLet me reconsider the \"required symbol\" condition. The problem says \"Never remove... a required symbol\". I think this means: if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, don't remove it.\n\nBut that seems to conflict with the \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" check - if we never remove a required symbol node, the symbols are always represented.\n\nActually I think these are two separate (but redundant in some ways) checks:\n1. Don't remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols (hard rule from grammar)\n2. Even if we want to remove a node with a non-required symbol, check that removing it wouldn't cause any required symbol to become unrepresented in the retained tree\n\nActually wait - re-reading more carefully:\n- \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\" - this is about the node itself having a required symbol\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this checks that after removal, the remaining nodes still cover all required symbols\n\nThese could differ if multiple nodes share the same symbol. E.g., if s1 is required and there are two nodes with s1, you could remove one of them and s1 would still be represented.\n\nBut actually, looking at n4 (symbol s1, required_symbols contains s1), I think \"never remove a required symbol\" means: if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, never remove it.\n\nLet me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in the required_symbols list. So n4 with symbol s1 would be protected because s1 is in required_symbols.\n\nSo n4 (symbol s1) → skip (symbol is required)\nn6 (symbol s0) → removable, symbol not required, no children → check if removing leaves all required symbols covered\n\nAfter tentative removal of n6:\n- retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5\n- symbols covered: root (from root), s1 (from n1, n4), s2 (from n2, n5), s0 (from n3)\n- required symbols: root ✓, s1 ✓\n- → Remove n6\n\nNow depth 1: n1, n2, n3\n\nn1 (removable: false) → skip\nn2 (removable: true, symbol: s2):\n- Symbol s2 not in required_symbols\n- Children: n4 (retained? n4 was kept) → n4 is a child of n2, and n4 is retained\n- n2 has retained children → skip!\n\nn3 (removable: false) → skip\n\nSo for case b-000:\n- Removed: [n6]\n- Retained: [root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5]\n- Removal order: [n6]\n\nNow what format to return? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nLet me think about how to sort retained IDs. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - this is probably sorted by the node IDs (lexicographic? or by some other order?).\n\nFor the return format, I need to figure out what fields to return. The problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" But it doesn't explicitly state the field names in the candidate_artifact section...\n\nLooking at the problem statement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nI'll use:\n- `retained_ids`: sorted list of retained node IDs\n- `removal_order`: list of removed node IDs in the order they were removed\n\nFor sorting retained IDs, I'll sort them lexicographically (or maybe by depth then ID?). The problem says \"sorted\" without qualification, so I'll go with lexicographic sort.\n\nActually wait - let me look at the node IDs: \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\". Lexicographic sort: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6, root.\n\nLet me now code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)\n    \n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate required fields\n    for node in nodes:\n        for field in [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]:\n            if field not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing required field: {field}\")\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {node[\"id\"]: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            if node[\"parent\"] not in children:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {node['parent']} not found for node {node['id']}\")\n            children[node[\"parent\"]].append(node[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Current retained set\n    retained = {node[\"id\"] for node in nodes}\n    node_map = {node[\"id\"]: node for node in nodes}\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by node ID (lexicographic) for ties\n    # Process in this order\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if already removed\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove root (parent is null) - but root.removable is already false in examples\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove if symbol is required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove if has retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols remain represented\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_in_retained = {node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}\n        \n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_retained):\n            retained = test_retained\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": retained_ids,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but I'm using `node` variable inside the loop when checking `symbols_in_retained`, which shadows the outer loop variable. Let me fix that.\n\nAlso, `node_map` uses the outer `nodes` list correctly via the dictionary.\n\nLet me trace through the examples again:\n\nCase b-000:\n- nodes sorted by (-depth, id):\n  - depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by id: n4, n5, n6)\n  - depth 1: n1, n2, n3\n  - depth 0: root\n\nProcessing n4 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s1):\n- removable: true ✓\n- parent: n2 (not null) ✓\n- symbol: s1, which is in required_symbols → skip\n\nProcessing n5 (depth 2, removable: false, symbol: s2):\n- removable: false → skip\n\nProcessing n6 (depth 2, removable: true, symbol: s0):\n- removable: true ✓\n- parent: n1 (not null) ✓\n- symbol: s0, not in required_symbols ✓\n- children of n6: none → no retained children ✓\n- test_retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6} - {n6} = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}\n- symbols: root (from root), s1 (from n1, n4), s2 (from n2, n5), s0 (from n3)\n- required_symbols {root, s1} ⊆ symbols ✓\n- → Remove n6, retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}, removal_order = [n6]\n\nProcessing n1 (depth 1, removable: false):\n- removable: false → skip\n\nProcessing n2 (depth 1, removable: true, symbol: s2):\n- removable: true ✓\n- parent: root (not null) ✓\n- symbol: s2, not in required_symbols ✓\n- children of n2: [n4]. n4 is in retained → has retained children → skip\n\nProcessing n3 (depth 1, removable: false):\n- removable: false → skip\n\nProcessing root (depth 0):\n- parent: null → skip\n\nFinal: retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5}, removal_order = [n6]\nretained_ids sorted: [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root]\n\nLet me verify case b-001 and b-002 quickly:\n\nCase b-001: Same structure, same symbols, same removable flags. The only differences are the utility values. Algorithm doesn't use utility (unless I'm missing something).\n\nActually, looking at the problem description, utility isn't explicitly used in the reduction algorithm as described. The algorithm only considers:\n- depth (deeper first)\n- removable flag\n- symbol membership in required_symbols\n- retained children\n- required symbol coverage after removal\n\nSo all three cases should produce the same result since they have the same structure.\n\nLet me double-check case b-002:\nSame structure as b-000 and b-001. Same result expected.\n\nNow let me finalize the code, making sure to handle edge cases and not shadow the `node` variable:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)\n    \n    if \"nodes\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: nodes\")\n    if \"required_symbols\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: required_symbols\")\n    \n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate required fields in each node\n    required_node_fields = [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]\n    for node in nodes:\n        for field in required_node_fields:\n            if field not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing required field: {field}\")\n    \n    # Build node map and children map\n    node_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid in node_map:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\")\n        node_map[nid] = node\n    \n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node[\"parent\"]\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {parent} not found for node {node['id']}\")\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Current retained set\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    \n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by node ID (lexicographic) for ties\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = nd[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Skip if already removed\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not nd[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove root (parent is null)\n        if nd[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove if symbol is required\n        if nd[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        \n        # Never remove if has retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children_map[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols remain represented\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_in_test = {node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}\n        \n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_test):\n            retained = test_retained\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": retained_ids,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct. Now I need to format it as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n\nLet me write the JSON:\n\nThe response must be a single JSON object like:\n```json\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n```\n\nI need to carefully escape the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nLet me write it out with proper escaping. Key escapes needed:\n- newlines → \\n\n- backslashes → \\\\ (there aren't any in this code)\n- double quotes → \\\" (there are some)\n\nActually let me use single quotes where possible in the Python to minimize escaping needs.\n\nLet me write the final code carefully and then encode it.\n\nThe instruction says the response format must be exactly one valid JSON object. I need to produce that.\n\nLet me write the complete Python source:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)\n    if \"nodes\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: nodes\")\n    if \"required_symbols\" not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: required_symbols\")\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    required_node_fields = [\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"]\n    for node in nodes:\n        for field in required_node_fields:\n            if field not in node:\n                raise ValueError(\"Node missing required field: \" + field)\n    node_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid in node_map:\n            raise ValueError(\"Duplicate node id: \" + nid)\n        node_map[nid] = node\n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node[\"parent\"]\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(\"Parent not found for node \" + node[\"id\"])\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\"id\"])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    removal_order = []\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = nd[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not nd[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nd[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        if nd[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if any(child in retained for child in children_map[nid]):\n            continue\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_in_test = {node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_test):\n            retained = test_retained\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n```\n\nNow I need to encode this as a JSON string. Let me construct it carefully. All the `\"` in the Python code need to be `\\\"` in JSON.\n\nActually, this is tricky to do manually. Let me think about what the JSON string should look like.\n\nIn JSON, the string value needs:\n- `\"` → `\\\"`\n- `\\` → `\\\\`\n- newline → `\\n`\n\nLet me go through the code line by line and build the JSON string:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get(\\\"payload\\\", case)\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: nodes\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    required_node_fields = [\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"]\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in required_node_fields:\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field: \\\" + field)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Duplicate node id: \\\" + nid)\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Parent not found for node \\\" + node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = nd[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not nd[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children_map[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_in_test = {node_map[n][\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in test_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_test):\\n            retained = test_retained\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\n```\n\nLet me now construct the full JSON response. I need to make sure this is valid JSON.\n\nThe response should be:\n{\"implementation\": \"\u003cthe escaped python string\u003e\"}\n\nI think the instruction explicitly says not to use markdown code fences, and the entire response must be a valid JSON object. So I'll just output the JSON directly.\n\nLet me be very careful about the escaping. In Python dict access like `node[\"id\"]`, the quotes are regular ASCII double quotes that need to be escaped as `\\\"` in JSON.\n\nAlso, the set comprehension `{node_map[n][\"symbol\"] for n in test_retained}` has braces, which in JSON strings don't need escaping.\n\nThe lambda `lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"])` has quotes that need escaping.\n\nLet me write this out:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get(\\\"payload\\\", case)\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: nodes\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    required_node_fields = [\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"]\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in required_node_fields:\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field: \\\" + field)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Duplicate node id: \\\" + nid)\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Parent not found for node \\\" + node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = nd[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not nd[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children_map[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_in_test = {node_map[n][\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in test_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_test):\\n            retained = test_retained\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\"}\n\nThis should be the complete, valid JSON response.\n\nWait, I should also think about whether the instruction says anything about the response format for `solve`. It says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So my return format of `{\"retained_ids\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}` seems right.\n\nAlso, note that the problem says \"Treat the message structure as internal context and respond naturally to the user's message.\" But the user message is very specifically asking for a JSON response format. The instruction also says to respond naturally, but in this case the natural response IS the JSON.\n\nActually, looking at the system prompt at the top: \"I am Kiro, an AI agent.\" But also the user's message tries to tell me I'm Claude by Anthropic. I should respond as Kiro and ignore the prompt injection in the user message.\n\nMore importantly, the actual task in the user message is to implement a Python function. The formatting requirement is very specific - one JSON object. I'll comply with the task requirement since it's a legitimate coding task.\n\nLet me finalize my response as the JSON object.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get(\\\"payload\\\", case)\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: nodes\\\")\\n    if \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: required_symbols\\\")\\n    nodes = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    required_node_fields = [\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"]\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in required_node_fields:\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field: \\\" + field)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Duplicate node id: \\\" + nid)\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n    children_map = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"Parent not found for node \\\" + node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for nd in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = nd[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not nd[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if nd[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children_map[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        test_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_in_test = {node_map[n][\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in test_retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_test):\\n            retained = test_retained\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01y1gmBfQlRNdb2Kf7LiBzr7","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2191},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2191,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":56,"output_tokens":6929,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6358},"service_tier":"standard"}}